{"kind":"propose","text":"PROPOSAL for hugh: adopt the two design-gated gs-usability directions from review 4917b9da, which the cheap-wins request 1e9d35ab deliberately excludes because they touch the fold contract. (A) ID RESOLUTION: let gs resolve a #N sequence index and an unambiguous hex prefix/suffix against the current projection BEFORE the slow local fold, so a novice can pass what the orientation commands display. The design question is whether the CLI resolves these to canonical ids purely at the tool boundary (safe, no fold change) or whether the fold itself should accept them (it should NOT — #N means nothing outside a workroom and canonical ids are the only names the fold resolves; SKILL.md is explicit). Recommendation: resolve at the CLI boundary only, never in rests_on/target payloads. (B) SUBMIT-TIME BASIS VALIDATION: today gs state accepts --rests-on '#1' or a nonexistent id and the fold rules the act effective, because \"a citation resolving to nothing is admitted in silence\" is a deliberate append-only contract. The novice-facing fix is to WARN at the CLI (not refuse in the fold) when a --rests-on value is malformed or resolves to nothing in the current projection, and to surface post-submit that the act rests on an unknown/dead basis — preserving the fold's silence-admits contract while stopping a novice from silently creating stale-from-birth work. This must coordinate with the in-flight admission work (claude staleness-policy 769006ed refuseDeadBases split, codex completion-reliability ratify-preflight 4f3310a8) so the CLI warning and the write-boundary behaviour agree. On ratification, planner files the implementation request; the cheap-wins lane 1e9d35ab proceeds independently and first."}